WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:04.799
In the late '90s, Matthew Lillard was slashing
his way into the big time with memorable appearances

00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:07.189
in Scream, Hackers, and She's All That.

00:00:07.189 --> 00:00:11.600
But before he ever managed hit the A-list,
his big screen career seemed to fizzle out.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:15.380
"My mom and dad are going to be so mad at
me."

00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:19.200
While he hasn't quite vanished from the film
scene, he's certainly endured his fair share

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:20.620
of cinematic setbacks.

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:23.640
So why did Lillard's star status seem to drop
so fast?

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.590
Here's why you don't hear much about Matthew
Lillard these days.

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:29.650
In the shadow of Stu

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:33.640
Lillard is probably best remembered for his
turn as Stu, the sidekick killer, in Wes Craven's

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:35.219
1996 classic Scream.

00:00:35.219 --> 00:00:40.070
By then, the actor had already turned some
heads with his oddball presence in the 1995

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:44.980
cyberdrama Hackers, but he stole so many scenes
in Scream with his goofy scare tactics that

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:46.309
his appeal was undeniable.

00:00:46.309 --> 00:00:51.289
"See we ask you a question and if you get
it wrong, bagah."

00:00:51.289 --> 00:00:54.989
The downside of etching that character in
viewers' memories, though, is that he's struggled

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:57.530
to establish a screen identity outside of
the role.

00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:01.649
And since Stu died in the first film, it's
not like he could return for any of its sequels.

00:01:01.649 --> 00:01:06.420
"Because let's face it baby, these days you
gotta have a sequel."

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:07.460
Lillard admitted to The A.V.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.510
Club that even two decades later, he's still
being asked about Stu, and said,

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:15.240
"Talking about Scream is like talking about
the first girl you ever kissed."

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:19.450
He added that he finds the continued attention
to the role "bizarre" and said he really doesn't

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.890
understand why it's held such a place in horror
fans' hearts.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:26.710
"Stop it Bea would ya alright, I can't anymore.

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:29.140
I'm feeling a little woozy here."

00:01:29.149 --> 00:01:32.549
But that's not the only role that's lingered
in Lillard's life …

00:01:32.549 --> 00:01:34.500
Long live Shaggy

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.740
After Scream, Lillard went on to star in several
roles that continued to showcase his signature

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:43.130
zaniness, like his dance-happy dirtbag role
in She's All That and as Stevo the punk rock

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:44.860
fan in SLC Punk!.

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:49.430
The movie that really set his career into
slower motion, though, was 2002's Scooby-Doo.

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:54.329
Lillard played the peculiar pet owner Shaggy
in the live-action film and its 2004 sequel,

00:01:54.329 --> 00:01:57.689
but even after that movie franchise ended,
Shaggy just stuck with him.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.900
"Now for the main course."

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:02.120
"No"

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:06.080
"You, ha ha ha."

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.330
Lillard credited the films with being the
biggest momentum killer of his career because

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.190
it "took [his] indie credit … and threw
it out the window."

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:16.220
But he's also been able to count on the subsequent
mini-movies, animated TV series, and video

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.420
games featuring the character for steady paychecks.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.030
In fact, Lillard admitted:

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:24.240
"If it wasn't for Scooby Doo, I wouldn't still
be in California acting, I'd be somewhere

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:25.500
else doing something else.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:29.670
Scooby Doo the movie saved my bacon, to be
honest—and it still does.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:33.780
That show is still around and it's the constant
job I have and I love doing the voice."

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:37.770
Even so, he's still an ensemble member in
that series, which is something he knows all

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:38.770
about …

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:41.340
Wingman central

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:45.290
With few exceptions, Matthew Lillard has almost
always been cast as the standout sidekick

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:49.400
character in his movies, especially when they
involved his once-frequent co-star Freddie

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:50.640
Prinze Jr.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.510
In fact, Lillard starred alongside Prinze
in a whopping five films, including She's

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:58.310
All That, Wing Commander, Summer Catch, and
both Scooby-Doo movies.

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:02.120
Their on- and off-screen bromance might have
worked well for a while, but even Lillard

00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.810
thinks it got to be overkill around the fourth
collaboration.

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:08.980
He once dubbed himself "Guy Who Was in a Freddie
Prinze Jr. Movie Too Many" and has vowed that

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.410
the pair won't be seen together on-screen
again.

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:17.150
"Yo yo, the beotch was like what, and I was
like, later on."

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:20.820
He didn't gain the reputation of being Prize's
second banana by design, of course.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.810
But
those few films which have given him a chance

00:03:22.810 --> 00:03:25.100
at the lead have been kinda regrettable.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:27.560
He dropped some real talk in an interview
with the A.V.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:28.560
Club, saying:

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:30.820
"The more lines I have, in general, the worse
a movie is.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:33.960
It's very rare that I get say great things
in fantastic movies.

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.400
So if you see me as like, number one on a
call sheet?

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.320
In general, that movie is pretty bad."

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.400
Speaking of which …

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:41.930
Certified rotten

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:46.150
Lillard's gone back and forth over which film
ranks his absolute worst — he's pointed

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:50.630
to both In the Name of the King: A Dungeon
Siege Tale and Home Run Showdown as some cringeworthy

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:55.760
installments on his résumé — but critics
have had a field day panning his pictures.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.760
His 1998 comedy-horror film Dead Man's Curve
got a giant goose egg of a Rotten Tomatoes

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:00.760
score.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:03.951
Then, there was Senseless, which lived up
to its own name and earned just 6% on the

00:04:03.951 --> 00:04:04.951
site.

00:04:04.951 --> 00:04:08.230
All told, there've been another half dozen
of his films that earned scathing reviews.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:12.870
While Lillard has starred in a few films that
critics liked, including Alexander Payne's

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:17.760
2011 hit The Descendants and his own 2012
directorial effort Fat Kid Rules the World,

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:21.930
the vast majority of his movies have been
major critical misses.

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:23.720
Missing the teen beat

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:27.660
Despite some early popularity and good looks,
Matthew Lillard wasn't ever really deemed

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:29.980
a heartthrob of the teen magazine scene.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:34.120
While actors like Leonardo DiCaprio, Jonathan
Taylor Thomas, and Josh Hartnett were literal

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:39.120
poster boys of the Teen Beat era, Lillard
wasn't tapped for the same kind of adoration.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.090
Because of that, he's never been promoted
to leading man status or given a rom-com role

00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:44.290
worth swooning over.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:48.410
He did have a romantic arc in 2004's Without
a Paddle, but that was a buddy comedy first

00:04:48.410 --> 00:04:50.950
and foremost — and a bad one, at that.

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:54.810
Considering how crucial that's been in other
successful careers in show business, it's

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:55.990
a bummer.

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:58.320
Small screen surprises

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:02.160
One field where Matthew Lillard has had some
luck is on the television circuit.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:06.940
Not only was he a series regular on FX's ambitious
crime drama The Bridge, but he's also appeared

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:10.320
on State of Affairs, The Good Wife, and Halt
and Catch Fire.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:15.400
He's also among the impressive cast of TV's
Twin Peaks revival, due to debut in 2017,

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:18.210
and revealed that his character in the show
will be "epic."

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:22.500
Moral of the story: He might not be a marquee
name at the local theater, but TV is giving

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:24.880
him some meat to chew on with his performances.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:28.480
"Just another beautiful day in paradise."

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:30.440
Thanks for watching!

